WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.260
It’s Professor Dave, I want to tell you
about superlatives.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:15.370
There are certain things that the Italian
language does that are fun and unique, like

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:18.169
the altered words we learned before.

00:00:18.169 --> 00:00:22.429
Another such construct is the way Italian
uses superlatives.

00:00:22.429 --> 00:00:26.689
It often does this by adding a suffix to a
particular adjective.

00:00:26.689 --> 00:00:31.230
But before we see these, let’s distinguish
between two types of superlatives.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:35.870
There is the absolute superlative, and the
relative superlative.

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:39.820
Let’s see an example of each.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.800
The first of these is an absolute superlative.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.460
It says that Fabio is very good.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:02.070
There is no specific limit to how good he is.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:05.740
Notice the suffix, issimo.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:09.009
The second example is a relative superlative.

00:01:09.009 --> 00:01:12.409
This is because his talent is limited to his school.

00:01:12.409 --> 00:01:20.460
The relative superlative is formed like the
comparative, but with an article in front.

00:01:41.229 --> 00:01:43.979
So let’s go back to the absolute superlative.

00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:47.140
Let’s practice forming these.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:51.319
If something is sweet, we would say that it is dolce.

00:01:51.319 --> 00:01:57.909
If we want to say that it is very sweet, we
could say molto dolce, but if we want to use

00:01:57.909 --> 00:02:02.770
another form of superlative, we could say
that it is dolcissimo.

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:06.740
Let’s try a few more.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:57.500
That’s how you make superlatives in Italian,
so let’s check comprehension.

